[Thinking]
We are going to create an input field with a search icon (reading glass) on the right side.
 The icon should be clickable and trigger an onClick function when clicked.

 Steps:
 1. We'll wrap the input and the icon in a container to position the icon absolutely on the right.
 2. The container should be relatively positioned so that we can absolutely position the icon inside.
 3. The input should have padding on the right to make space for the icon.
 4. The icon (SVG) will be placed inside the container and positioned absolutely to the right.
 5. We'll set the icon as clickable (with cursor: pointer) and attach the onClick handler.

 HTML structure:
   <div class="input-container">
     <input type="text" class="search-input" />
     <svg class="search-icon" ...> ... </svg>
   </div>

 CSS:
   .input-container {
     position: relative;
     display: inline-block;
   }

   .search-input {
     padding-right: 30px; /* enough space for the icon */
     width: 200px; /* or whatever width */
   }

   .search-icon {
     position: absolute;
     right: 8px; /* adjust as needed */
     top: 50%;
     transform: translateY(-50%);
     cursor: pointer;
     width: 16px;
     height: 16px;
     /* you might want to set color or use fill */
   }

 6. In the onClick handler of the SVG, we can call the desired function.

 However, note: if we are using React, we would handle the click in the React way.

 Let me write a React component example:

 Example in React:

   const SearchInput = ({ onSearch }) => {
     return (
       <div style={{ position: 'relative', display: 'inline-block' }}>
         <input
           type="text"
           placeholder="Search..."
           style={{ paddingRight: '30px', width: '200px' }}
           // If you also want to trigger on enter key press, you can add onKeyDown
         />
         <svg
           style={{
             position: 'absolute',
             right: '8px',
             top: '50%',
             transform: 'translateY(-50%)',
             cursor: 'pointer',
             width: '16px',
             height: '16px'
           }}
           onClick={onSearch}
           viewBox="0 0 24 24"
           fill="none"
           stroke="currentColor"
           strokeWidth="2"
           strokeLinecap="round"
           strokeLinejoin="round"
         >
           <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8" />
           <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65" />
         </svg>
       </div>
     );
   };

 But note: the above inline styles are for illustration. Better to use CSS classes.

 Alternatively, if we are not in React, we can do it with plain HTML and JavaScript:

   HTML:
     <div class="input-container">
       <input type="text" id="searchInput" placeholder="Search...">
       <svg class="search-icon" id="searchIcon" ...> ... </svg>
     </div>

   JavaScript:
     document.getElementById('searchIcon').addEventListener('click', function() {
       const value = document.getElementById('searchInput').value;
       // do something with value
     });

 Let me provide a complete plain HTML example.

 However, the question says: "after I click it after putting something in input function is called onClick"

 So, we are going to call a function that uses the input value.

 We'll do:

   function performSearch() {
     const input = document.getElementById('searchInput');
     const query = input.value;
     // ... do something with query
   }

   Then, in the click handler for the icon, call performSearch.

 But note: the question says the icon is clickable and after putting something in input, so we get the value at click time.

 Let's write the plain HTML/JS version and the React version.

 Since the question does not specify the framework, I'll provide both.

