Here's a complete solution using plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript that positions the search icon inside the input field on the right side, makes it clickable, and triggers a function when clicked:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Search Input with Icon</title>
  <style>
    .search-container {
      position: relative;
      display: inline-block;
      width: 300px;
    }

    .search-input {
      width: 100%;
      padding: 10px 40px 10px 15px;
      border: 1px solid #ddd;
      border-radius: 4px;
      font-size: 16px;
      box-sizing: border-box;
    }

    .search-icon {
      position: absolute;
      right: 12px;
      top: 50%;
      transform: translateY(-50%);
      width: 20px;
      height: 20px;
      cursor: pointer;
      transition: opacity 0.2s;
      fill: #666;
    }

    .search-icon:hover {
      opacity: 0.8;
      fill: #000;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="search-container">
    <input 
      type="text" 
      class="search-input" 
      id="searchInput"
      placeholder="Search..."
    >
    <svg class="search-icon" id="searchIcon" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <!-- Search icon (magnifying glass) -->
      <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>
      <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"/>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <script>
    document.getElementById('searchIcon').addEventListener('click', function() {
      const searchValue = document.getElementById('searchInput').value.trim();

      // Your search logic here
      if(searchValue) {
        console.log('Searching for:', searchValue);
        performSearch(searchValue);
      } else {
        alert('Please enter a search term');
      }
    });

    // Handle Enter key press in input
    document.getElementById('searchInput').addEventListener('keypress', function(e) {
      if (e.key === 'Enter') {
        const searchValue = this.value.trim();
        if(searchValue) {
          performSearch(searchValue);
        }
      }
    });

    function performSearch(query) {
      // Your actual search implementation goes here
      alert(`Searching for: ${query}`);

      // Example: Send to server
      // fetch(`/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`)
      //   .then(response => response.json())
      //   .then(data => console.log(data));
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Key Features:

1. **Positioned Icon**:
   - The SVG search icon is absolutely positioned inside the input container
   - Appears on the right side of the input field
   - Properly centered vertically using `transform: translateY(-50%)`

2. **Clickable Icon**:
   - Cursor changes to pointer on hover
   - Hover effect (slight darkening) for visual feedback
   - Click triggers the search function

3. **Functionality**:
   - Gets input value on icon click
   - Trims whitespace from input
   - Validates empty input
   - Handles both icon click and Enter key press
   - Includes sample search function (`performSearch`)

4. **Responsive Design**:
   - Proper padding on input to make space for icon
   - Container uses relative units
   - Mobile-friendly touch target

### How to Use:

1. Copy the entire code into an HTML file
2. When you:
   - Type something in the input field and click the search icon
   - Or press Enter after typing
3. It will:
   - Validate the input isn't empty
   - Call `performSearch()` with the search term
   - Show an alert (replace with your actual search logic)

### Customization Options:

1. **Icon Color**:
   - Change `fill: #666` in `.search-icon` to your preferred color
   - Modify hover color in `.search-icon:hover`

2. **Sizing**:
   - Adjust width/height in `.search-icon`
   - Modify padding in `.search-input` if changing icon size

3. **Search Logic**:
   - Replace the alert in `performSearch()` with your actual implementation
   - Add API calls, form submissions, etc.

4. **Styling**:
   - Change border color/radius in `.search-input`
   - Adjust container width as needed
   - Modify hover effects

This solution works in all modern browsers and includes both mouse click and keyboard (Enter key) support for accessibility. The search icon remains visible at all times, providing clear user cues about the search functionality.

